{
  "teamId": 112,
  "year": 2026,
  "narrative": "The 2026 Chicago Cubs came out of the gates with a burst of energy that immediately signaled this would be a competitive ballclub. In mid-April, Alex Bregman announced himself as a cornerstone of the lineup with a clutch single that set the tone, and Michael Busch delivered a two-run knock the very next day. The offense was electric from the jump, with Michael Conforto providing big hits and young catcher Moisés Ballesteros stunning fans with a grand slam that showed the Cubs had genuine depth throughout their batting order.\n\nMay and early June belonged to Pete Crow-Armstrong, who emerged as the team's most electric performer. A triple, a home run, and a go-ahead blast in consecutive games in early May announced PCA's arrival as a legitimate star. He followed that with an 11th homer in June, and his bat showed up repeatedly in clutch moments alongside Dansby Swanson and Seiya Suzuki. This stretch powered the Cubs to their best stretch of baseball and firmly planted them in playoff contention, building a cushion that would prove essential.\n\nThe summer, however, brought turbulence. Jacob Webb became the story nobody wanted to tell — a reliever who surrendered walk-off or game-changing home runs with alarming regularity, giving up crushing shots on August 22nd and 25th that each carried WPA swings approaching or exceeding 0.85. Ryan Rolison and Caleb Thielbar added their own ugly moments to the ledger. Yet every time the bullpen threatened to sink the season, the lineup responded — Ian Happ's grand slam on August 23rd, Alex Bregman's clutch August hitting, and Nico Hoerner's timely singles kept the Cubs afloat and ultimately across the 89-win threshold.\n\nOctober arrived with hope but delivered heartbreak. Matthew Boyd took the Wild Card mound and was touched up by Fernando Tatis Jr., Manny Machado, and Xander Bogaerts in a performance that never gave the Cubs a fighting chance. The offense couldn't manufacture enough against San Diego's veteran lineup, and the season ended one round too soon. It was a franchise that showed real promise — 850 runs, 220 home runs, and a cast of young stars — but the bullpen fragility that haunted them all summer ultimately followed them into autumn.",
  "chapters": [
